.TH CSORT_SORT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
csort_sort \- sort any collection with custom getter and comparison
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <csort.h>

bool sorted = csort_sort(collection, length, elem_size, getter_proc,
                          comparison_proc, mprocs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Generic sorting macro that sorts any collection using an iterative
mergesort algorithm. Works with any collection type (arrays, vectors,
custom containers) as long as getter and comparison functions are provided.

This is a wrapper macro around ___csort_merge_sort().
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
collection       Pointer to collection to sort
length          Number of elements in collection
elem_size       Size of each element in bytes
getter_proc     Function to get element at index
comparison_proc Function to compare two elements
mprocs          Memory management procedures, or NULL for default; if
                non-NULL, malloc/free/calloc/realloc must all be set
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns true on success (including the trivial cases of a NULL
collection, a length of 0 or 1, or an elem_size of 0, none of which
have anything to do).
Returns false if length exceeds the library's maximum element count,
mprocs is non-NULL but does not have all four of malloc/free/calloc/
realloc populated, the internal temporary merge buffer could not be
allocated, or if length * elem_size would overflow size_t; the
collection is left completely untouched in that case.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stable sort (preserves relative order of equal elements)
O(n log n) time complexity in all cases
O(n) space complexity for temporary merge buffer
Iterative implementation (no recursion)

getter_proc and comparison_proc must both be non-NULL whenever
collection is non-NULL and length is 2 or greater. This holds even
when elem_size is 0, a case where neither one would actually end up
being called. Only a NULL collection or a length of 0 or 1 exempts a
call from needing genuine, non-NULL procs. Violating this asserts
(aborts the process); it does not return false.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
// Sort a plain array
int arr[] = {3, 1, 2};
void *getter(void *col, size_t i) { return &((int*)col)[i]; }
int cmp(const void *a, const void *b) {
  return (*(int*)a > *(int*)b) - (*(int*)a < *(int*)b);
}
csort_sort(arr, 3, sizeof(int), getter, cmp, NULL);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR csort_get_default_comparison_proc (3),
.BR cvec_sort (3)
